## 2026-10-04

### Disk mounts containing whitespace reach the DISK card (#41)

**Greyforge Labs** · GitHub [@GreyforgeLabs](https://github.com/GreyforgeLabs) · X [@GreyforgeLabs](https://x.com/GreyforgeLabs)

`parseDfRows()` split each `df` row on whitespace, so a mount path containing
a space never reached the disk card. Collection still queries only `/` and the
home directory; a removable drive mounted somewhere else is not listed. Among
the filesystems already queried, the row is now matched as a path plus three
trailing numeric columns. The header is still skipped. The first valid row for
a full path is kept, a rejected row does not block a later valid row for that
mount, and that later row does not replace a different mount already kept.
Same-size, same-used pools still show once.

Reviewed here before merge: the old and new functions run over live
`df -B1 --output=target,size,used,avail` text return identical rows for every
mount without a space. 578 tests pass. X profile verified from the GitHub
profile field and social accounts; already credited in the blip CHANGE.log.

## 2026-09-30

### Web Mode: the desk in a browser over Tailscale or manual HTTPS (#25)

**TechLuddite** · GitHub [@TechLuddite](https://github.com/TechLuddite) · X [@gh_TechLuddite](https://x.com/gh_TechLuddite)

The desk renders as a page for a phone or a second machine. Two access modes
only, both encrypted: Tailscale Serve, and manual HTTPS with an existing
certificate. Off by default, and turning it on takes an explicit, persisted
true.

**LAN HTTP is gone, as asked at review.** It bound 0.0.0.0:8787 with no TLS and
carried the bearer token in the URL path, so the desk and a live credential
crossed the network in cleartext. The backend is loopback-only behind Tailscale
Serve; manual HTTPS serves its own pinned certificate; a saved "lan" mode loads
with WEB off and starts nothing, and there is no plain-HTTP fallback anywhere.

Also fixed from the review, each as its own commit:
- viewer links minted while LAN HTTP existed are replaced once, under a lock,
  on the grounds they may already have crossed the wire (CONFIG_VERSION 2).
- `url` prints the token id and last four characters; the full link needs
  `--reveal`, so a status check cannot leave a live credential in scrollback.
- token-revoke now tells "no such id" from "that is the last token".
- privacy default matches #17: only a saved literal true masks.
- the SETTINGS drawer fits 1600x1000 in three columns with no page scroll,
  and the test prints the measurement: content 755 against a 755 viewport.

Merged on top of #16. Two conflicts, both taken as unions: the InfoView resolve
ceiling (#16's +6 and this PR's +3 over the same base, so 602 with both reasons
kept) and the InfoModel line in Infomarchy.qml, which now carries #16's
usageVisible and this PR's webEnabled in `active`.

555 tests pass on the merged tree. Fifth contribution from this author.

### REMOTE: an optional roster file becomes a read-only card (#13)

**Wulf Kaal** · GitHub [@wulfkaal](https://github.com/wulfkaal) · X [@WulfKaal](https://x.com/WulfKaal)

Agents running somewhere this machine cannot see (a VPS, a colleague's box, a
CI runner) get a card, as long as something over there drops a JSON roster file
and `INFOMARCHY_REMOTE_ROSTER` points at it. No roster variable means no card,
no file read, no network. The collector reads that file on its own tick: nothing
reaches across a network to build a card, which is the promise the README makes.

Read-only on purpose. A remote agent has no pid here, nothing to focus and
nothing to stop, so the card has no per-row actions at all. Its single action is
an optional workspace number to jump to.

Rebased by me, not by the author, after eight days with no reply and 82 commits
of drift. Fred's call. His authorship is kept; the conflicts were entirely
master moving underneath:
- the right-column order gained FLEET (#35) and CONTAINERS (#19) since this was
  written, so `remoteRoster` joins the full list rather than replacing it, and
  sits after LOCAL AI where the review asked for it. His own migration function
  needed no change: it appends whatever is missing and then inserts remoteRoster
  after localAi, so the seven-card list migrates correctly.
- `collector.ts` kept gitea, fleet and the roster in the snapshot.
- `InfoModel.qml` kept both the fleet-session jump (#37) and his focusWorkspace.
- the InfoView resolve ceiling went 602 to 609 for the card's delegate.
- his layout differential test evaluated bindings that have since become
  multi-line and gained a `dense` flag; the helper now reads both from source,
  so the test still compares what the desk actually computes.

Verified live rather than only in tests: with no variable set the snapshot has
no roster at all; pointed at a file it produced the counts and the NEEDS YOU row;
and a roster line carrying `GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_...` came out as `[redacted]`.

576 tests pass on the merged tree. Second contribution from this author (after #12).

### Local usage collection, an optional billing meter, and HARD REFRESH (#16)

**TechLuddite** · GitHub [@TechLuddite](https://github.com/TechLuddite) · X [@gh_TechLuddite](https://x.com/gh_TechLuddite)

HARD REFRESH is the first chip in the module strip, with IPC for the desk and
the overlay; it bypasses the GitHub, external-IP and Grok billing caches. Grok
token totals now come from Grok's own local session files when Omarchy has no
record for it, and USAGE renders each provider as its own tinted block.

The whole 09-18 review came back as one commit per item, and all of it checks out
here:
- Both outbound paths are opt-in now, enable flags instead of skip flags:
  `INFOMARCHY_ALLOW_GROK_BILLING=1` and `INFOMARCHY_ALLOW_CLAUDE_REFRESH=1`.
  Nothing spends against an AI account unless asked.
- Both are gated on the USAGE card being on screen, passed as the `--usage-visible`
  argv flag rather than an environment variable, so an inherited environment cannot
  claim the card is visible. Proven live: flag alone is false, card alone is false,
  both together is true.
- HARD REFRESH no longer bypasses the Claude spend throttle. Forcing skips caches,
  never the limiter, and a forced Grok billing fetch still waits 10 seconds.
- The declarative collector command is back, so a future `collector.running = true`
  cannot silently drop `--demo`, and the unreachable return in
  `grokCliAccessToken()` is gone.

477 tests pass on the merged tree. Fourth contribution from this author.

## 2026-09-22

### Event-driven refresh on top of the poll (#39)

**Reverb** · GitHub [@reverb256](https://github.com/reverb256) · X: none found (checked 2026-09-18, see Backfill)

The follow-up Fred invited on #11, built to the constraints he set there: the
repeating timer stays as the floor, and paths come from HOME, HERMES_HOME and
XDG_STATE_HOME with no literal home directory. A change to Hermes's state.db or
WAL, or to dashboard.json, pulls the next refresh forward after 250 ms.

Fixed in the merge: FileView preloads by default, which would have read gus's
12 MB state.db and 1 MB WAL into memory in every desk instance only to watch
them. Adding `preload: false` keeps them as pure watches. A throwaway quickshell
confirmed the change signal still fires on append and atomic replace without
loading the file. Dropped `atomicWrites` from the dashboard.json watcher,
since it only affects writes and this FileView never writes.

435 tests pass on the merged tree. Second contribution (after #11).

### Pin the desk to one workspace, keeping the wallpaper on all of them (#38)

**Casey Shields** · GitHub [@shieldsworks](https://github.com/shieldsworks) · X: none found (checked 2026-09-22)

`omarchy-shell infomarchy setDeskWorkspace 5` shows the cards only while
workspace 5 is on screen. The wallpaper still draws everywhere, because the gate
sits on the cards and not the background surface. `0` (the default) means every
workspace, so an install that never sets it behaves exactly as before. Values
out of range are refused, not clamped.

Merged on top of #37 and #40. The Infomarchy.qml resolve ceiling conflict with
#40 was taken as a union (26 + 4 + 2 = 32). 435 tests pass on the merged tree.
First contribution from this author.

Note on the handle: GitHub has no X field and no social accounts, and
